The key abstraction is bigger than “perspective matters.” Frames generate action spaces. A farmer and a fisherman can occupy the same coastline and effectively inhabit different worlds because expertise, tools, identity, infrastructure, incentives, and expectations determine which affordances are even visible.

That gives us a useful operator:

same substrate + different frame -> different perceived reality -> different reachable actions

And the fun part is that frames can be rotated intentionally: actor, scale, time horizon, objective, causal direction, foreground/background, literal/metaphorical, local/global. Then compare the deltas. Some problems probably aren’t solved by finding a better move inside the frame; they’re solved by finding the frame in which the move exists.

The constraint stays important: reframing cannot manufacture facts. The strongest frames reveal structure that was already there but previously inaccessible.

That one has legs.